{
  "id": "section-progressive",
  "moduleId": "verb-tenses",
  "label": "Section 3",
  "title": "Continuous / Progressive",
  "summary": "Describe actions in progress with -고 있어, mark where actions happen with 에서, and compare all four forms.",
  "learningObjectives": [
    "Form the progressive with stem + 고 있어",
    "Tell the difference between habits (present) and actions underway (progressive)",
    "Use 에서 to mark where an action happens, and contrast it with 에",
    "Convert one verb across present, past, future, and progressive"
  ],
  "newVocabularyIds": ["read", "write", "work", "wait", "exercise", "book", "letter", "home", "now", "here"],
  "reviewVocabularyIds": ["study", "korean-language", "friend", "i-me", "tomorrow", "yesterday"],
  "grammarConceptIds": ["progressive-go-isseo", "location-eseo", "present-casual", "past-casual", "future-eul-geoya"],
  "estimatedMinutes": 25,
  "steps": [
    {
      "id": "s3-intro",
      "type": "intro",
      "title": "Talking about what you're doing right now",
      "body": [
        "Use -고 있어 to describe an action in progress: 공부하고 있어 (I'm studying).",
        "Depending on context, it can mean an action happening at this moment, or an activity that's ongoing around now, like a project you're in the middle of.",
        "You'll learn five daily-action verbs, the particle 에서 for where something happens, and then compare all four forms you know."
      ],
      "callout": "-고 있어 describes something underway. It's not a future form: English says \"I'm meeting a friend tomorrow,\" but Korean uses 만날 거야 for that."
    },
    {
      "id": "s3-vocab-verbs",
      "type": "vocabulary",
      "title": "New words: five daily actions",
      "vocabularyIds": ["read", "write", "work", "wait", "exercise"]
    },
    {
      "id": "s3-vocab-nouns",
      "type": "vocabulary",
      "title": "New words: things, places, and time",
      "vocabularyIds": ["book", "letter", "home", "now", "here"]
    },
    {
      "id": "s3-grammar-progressive",
      "type": "grammar",
      "title": "Progressive: stem + 고 있어",
      "grammarConceptIds": ["progressive-go-isseo"],
      "body": [
        "This is the easiest ending yet: remove 다 and add 고 있어. The stem never changes, whether it ends in a vowel or a consonant.",
        "있어 is the casual present of 있다 (to be/exist), so -고 있어 literally means something like \"am in the middle of doing.\""
      ],
      "rules": [
        { "pattern": "stem + 고 있어", "explanation": "공부하 + 고 있어 → 공부하고 있어, 읽 + 고 있어 → 읽고 있어" }
      ],
      "forms": [
        { "dictionary": "공부하다", "vocabularyId": "study", "form": "공부하고 있어", "romanization": "gongbuhago isseo", "english": "am studying", "parts": [{ "text": "공부하", "kind": "stem" }, { "text": "고 있어", "kind": "ending" }] },
        { "dictionary": "읽다", "vocabularyId": "read", "form": "읽고 있어", "romanization": "ilgo isseo", "english": "am reading", "parts": [{ "text": "읽", "kind": "stem" }, { "text": "고 있어", "kind": "ending" }], "derivation": "Spoken roughly as [일꼬 이써]: the ㄱ in ㄺ goes silent before ㄱ. The spelling doesn't change." },
        { "dictionary": "쓰다", "vocabularyId": "write", "form": "쓰고 있어", "romanization": "sseugo isseo", "english": "am writing", "parts": [{ "text": "쓰", "kind": "stem" }, { "text": "고 있어", "kind": "ending" }] },
        { "dictionary": "일하다", "vocabularyId": "work", "form": "일하고 있어", "romanization": "ilhago isseo", "english": "am working", "parts": [{ "text": "일하", "kind": "stem" }, { "text": "고 있어", "kind": "ending" }] },
        { "dictionary": "기다리다", "vocabularyId": "wait", "form": "기다리고 있어", "romanization": "gidarigo isseo", "english": "am waiting", "parts": [{ "text": "기다리", "kind": "stem" }, { "text": "고 있어", "kind": "ending" }] },
        { "dictionary": "운동하다", "vocabularyId": "exercise", "form": "운동하고 있어", "romanization": "undonghago isseo", "english": "am exercising", "parts": [{ "text": "운동하", "kind": "stem" }, { "text": "고 있어", "kind": "ending" }] }
      ],
      "callout": {
        "title": "Present or progressive?",
        "body": [
          "Present for habits and general actions: 나는 책을 읽어. = I read books.",
          "Progressive for an action underway: 나는 지금 책을 읽고 있어. = I'm reading a book now."
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "s3-grammar-eseo",
      "type": "grammar",
      "title": "Where it happens: 에서",
      "grammarConceptIds": ["location-eseo", "destination-e"],
      "body": [
        "에서 marks the place where an action happens: 집에서 일해 (I work at home).",
        "Compare it with 에 from Section 2, which marks where you're going: 집에 가 (I go home).",
        "With 여기 (here), 여기에서 is usually shortened to 여기서."
      ],
      "rules": [
        { "pattern": "place + 에서 + action", "explanation": "집에서 운동해 = exercise at home" },
        { "pattern": "place + 에 + 가다/오다", "explanation": "집에 가 = go home" },
        { "pattern": "여기에서 → 여기서", "explanation": "여기서 기다려 = wait here" }
      ],
      "examples": [
        {
          "id": "s3-eseo-demo",
          "korean": "나는 집에서 책을 읽어.",
          "romanization": "Naneun jibeseo chaegeul ilgeo.",
          "english": "I read books at home.",
          "tokens": [
            { "text": "나는", "romanization": "naneun", "meaning": "I (topic)", "role": "topic", "marker": "는", "vocabularyId": "i-me", "grammarConceptId": "topic-eun-neun" },
            { "text": "집에서", "romanization": "jibeseo", "meaning": "at home (where the action happens)", "role": "location", "marker": "에서", "vocabularyId": "home", "grammarConceptId": "location-eseo" },
            { "text": "책을", "romanization": "chaegeul", "meaning": "book (object)", "role": "object", "marker": "을", "vocabularyId": "book", "grammarConceptId": "object-eul-reul" },
            { "text": "읽어", "romanization": "ilgeo", "meaning": "read", "role": "verb", "marker": "어", "vocabularyId": "read", "grammarConceptId": "present-casual" }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
